[2026-10-23] r:5624648052 review COMMENTED by fametrano (contributor) (edited 2026-10-23):
`ReconstructMultipath` accepts relatives that repeat a path value, and then returns a descriptor that is not their multipath form.

I tested it with a small harness against btclib-wallet, a BIP389 implementation I maintain. Its expansions of 24 multipath descriptors each came back as the original. Thirteen mismatched sets (different keys, function, threshold, timelock, key order, tree shape, origin, wildcard or range, two changed steps in one key, musig steps at both levels) return null, as they should.

Repeated values are not caught. `wpkh(X/0/*)`, `wpkh(X/1/*)`, `wpkh(X/0/*)` gives `wpkh(X/<0;1;0>/*)`, which `Parse` refuses as a duplicate. `wpkh(X/0/*)` twice and then `wpkh(X/1/*)` gives `wpkh(X/<0;1>/*)`: two elements for three inputs. Two identical inputs give the single-path descriptor back. In `BIP32PubkeyProvider::ReconstructMultipath`, each relative is compared with `p` after `AddMultipathIndex` has already extended it. So a repeat is skipped while `p` still holds one index, and appended once it holds more. Nothing checks the indexes already in `p`, or that the step ends with one index per input. The inline suggestions add both checks, with a test for the repeated cases, musig included. I can share the harness and its outputs.
